WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.490
ED GLAESER: A major theme of this course is that cities are people, not buildings.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.770
The real city is the connected humanity that inhabits skyscrapers,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.910
not the skyscrapers themselves, but still, the skyscrapers matter.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.596
And so does all of the built environment that defines a city's skyline.

00:00:15.596 --> 00:00:17.862
A city's buildings give it form and beauty, and

00:00:17.862 --> 00:00:21.580
they determine the city's affordability and access to jobs.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.320
Buildings have a lasting impact on cities, and a better understanding of the built

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.740
environment enables us to better understand everything else about the city.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.700
The first question about the physical city is just why does the city look the way it does?

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.890
Why do the structures on Chicago's Lake Shore Drive soar to the sky, and

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.160
why is Paris comparatively flat?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.700
Why is Mumbai crowded but

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.900
short, while Singapore has dispersed high rise dwellings?

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.820
Why is it so easy to find your way around New York, and so

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.490
easy to get lost in Sao Paulo?

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.640
The answer to why a city has a certain shape can often lie in the answer to

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.020
a second question.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.080
How do buildings impact the experience of urban life?

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.090
These two questions are so

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:02.235
related because buildings often come about to solve a particular urban problem.

00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:06.057
The dense worn streets in older European cities were meant to enable

00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:08.360
transportation during a pedestrian era.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.040
And they still work better for walking than for driving.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.040
The third question is how should governments regulate urban form?

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.560
Is there a right answer, a grid for every city, or

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.180
should modern cities be a little less monotonous.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.540
Is it right to limit height or

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.400
to limit sprawl, the built environment may not be the city.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.404
But Churchill was right,

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:31.740
that first we shape our buildings thereafter they shape us.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.790
Understanding both skyscrapers and

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.440
housing in slums is a crucial part of understanding our cities.

